# Add Webview Panel

## Purpose

Use this skill when creating a new webview panel in the xcaffold VS Code extension. Webview panels extend `BaseWebview`, use nonce-based Content Security Policy, and access CLI data through the `DataSource` interface.

## Prerequisites

- xcaffold-vscode repo checked out and `npm install` completed
- `npm run typecheck` passing on the current branch
- `npm run test` passing on the current branch
- Familiar with `src/webview/baseWebview.ts` and `src/webview/dataSource.ts`

## Steps

### 1. Create the provider file

Create `src/<name>Provider.ts` extending `BaseWebview`. Name the file using the panel's function in camelCase, for example `fidelityProvider.ts` or `statusDashProvider.ts`.

```ts
import * as vscode from 'vscode';
import { BaseWebview } from './webview/baseWebview';
import { DataSource } from './webview/dataSource';

export class <Name>Provider extends BaseWebview {
    constructor(
        context: vscode.ExtensionContext,
        private readonly dataSource: DataSource
    ) {
        super(context, '<name>', '<Panel Title>');
    }

    protected getHtml(webview: vscode.Webview): string {
        const nonce = this.getNonce();
        const scriptUri = webview.asWebviewUri(
            vscode.Uri.joinPath(this._context.extensionUri, 'out', '<name>.js')
        );
        return `<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy"
        content="default-src 'none';
                 script-src 'nonce-${nonce}';
                 style-src 'nonce-${nonce}';
                 img-src ${webview.cspSource};
                 font-src ${webview.cspSource};">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title><Panel Title></title>
</head>
<body>
    <div id="root"></div>
    <script nonce="${nonce}" src="${scriptUri}"></script>
</body>
</html>`;
    }

    protected handleMessage(message: unknown): void {
        // Handle postMessage replies from the webview context
    }
}
```

### 2. Implement getHtml(webview)

`getHtml` must return HTML that satisfies the CSP contract:

- Call `this.getNonce()` at the top — use the returned value for every inline `<script nonce="...">` and `<style nonce="...">` tag
- Load scripts via `webview.asWebviewUri()` — never use bare file paths or external CDN URLs
- The CSP template is: `default-src 'none'; script-src 'nonce-{nonce}'; style-src 'nonce-{nonce}'; img-src ${webview.cspSource}; font-src ${webview.cspSource};`
- Never use `unsafe-inline` in any CSP directive

### 3. Implement handleMessage(message)

`handleMessage` receives messages sent from the webview context via `window.acquireVsCodeApi().postMessage(...)`. Parse the message type and dispatch to the appropriate action:

```ts
protected handleMessage(message: unknown): void {
    if (typeof message !== 'object' || message === null) { return; }
    const msg = message as { type: string; payload?: unknown };
    switch (msg.type) {
        case 'requestData':
            this.loadAndSend();
            break;
    }
}
```

### 4. Create the CLI output parser

Extract CLI output parsing into a **pure function** at the top of the file. This function takes a raw string (stdout from the CLI) and returns a typed result. Keep it separate from the class so it can be unit-tested without a VS Code host.

```ts
// Pure function — testable without VS Code
export function parse<Name>Output(raw: string): <OutputType> {
    // Parse JSON or text output from xcaffold CLI
    // Return a typed result or throw on malformed input
}
```

### 5. Register the command in extension.ts

Add the command registration inside `activate()`:

```ts
const <name>Provider = new <Name>Provider(context, dataSource);
context.subscriptions.push(<name>Provider);

const <name>Command = vscode.commands.registerCommand(
    'xcaffold.<name>',
    () => <name>Provider.reveal()
);
context.subscriptions.push(<name>Command);
```

### 6. Add command to package.json

Add the command to the `contributes.commands` array:

```json
{
    "command": "xcaffold.<name>",
    "title": "<Panel Title>",
    "category": "Xcaffold"
}
```

Optionally add a keybinding or menu entry under `contributes.menus` if the command should appear in the command palette or editor context menu.

### 7. Add to viewsWelcome (optional)

If the panel should appear in the xcaffold sidebar view, add a `viewsWelcome` entry in package.json pointing to this command. Only do this for panels that represent a primary workflow surface.

### 8. Create the test file

Create `src/test/<name>Provider.test.ts`. Test the pure parser function directly:

```ts
import * as assert from 'assert';
import { parse<Name>Output } from '../../<name>Provider';

suite('<Name>Provider', () => {
    test('parses valid CLI output', () => {
        const raw = '{"status": "ok"}';
        const result = parse<Name>Output(raw);
        assert.strictEqual(result.status, 'ok');
    });

    test('throws on malformed output', () => {
        assert.throws(() => parse<Name>Output('not json'), /SyntaxError/);
    });
});
```

### 9. Run verification

Both commands must exit 0 before the work is considered complete:

```bash
npm run typecheck   # tsc --noEmit
npm run test        # Mocha suite
```

## Anti-Patterns

| Anti-Pattern | Why It Fails |
|---|---|
| Using `unsafe-inline` in CSP | Security defect — allows XSS via injected scripts |
| Referencing external CDN URLs in script-src or style-src | No external network allowed in webview context |
| Loading scripts by absolute file path instead of asWebviewUri() | Webview context blocks file:// URIs; extension resources must be converted |
| Reusing the same nonce across requests | Nonce must be unique per response — call getNonce() fresh each time getHtml() is called |
| Putting CLI parsing logic inside the class | Cannot unit-test without VS Code host; pure function extraction is required |
| Calling DataSource methods in the constructor | Data fetching must happen in response to user action or a message, not at construction time |
| Adding a runtime `dependencies` entry in package.json | Extension ships with zero runtime deps |
